WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.792
The tool runner, or your own loop.

2
00:00:02.072 --> 00:00:06.744
The Anthropic SDKs include a tool runner (in beta at the time of writing).

3
00:00:07.024 --> 00:00:19.611
You write ordinary functions, decorate them, and the runner generates the schemas, calls the API, runs your functions when the model asks, feeds results back, and stops when the model stops calling tools.

4
00:00:19.891 --> 00:00:26.760
When a tool raises an exception, the runner returns it to the model as an error result, the same pattern you just learned.

5
00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:38.517
You can cap it with a maximum number of iterations, and you can intervene on each turn: inspect the pending tool calls, take over the message history, or modify results before they go back.

6
00:00:38.797 --> 00:00:40.056
So which should you use?

7
00:00:40.336 --> 00:00:51.984
The docs give both answers depending on the page: the runner for most custom-tool agents, and the manual loop when you want complete control over approval, logging, or conditional execution.

8
00:00:52.264 --> 00:00:59.091
A reasonable rule: write the manual loop once, by hand, so you know what the runner is doing (that is this week’s lab).

9
00:00:59.371 --> 00:01:08.715
In production, use the runner when its hooks cover your needs, and keep a manual loop when you want to own every step, or when you would rather not depend on a beta helper.

10
00:01:08.995 --> 00:01:12.877
Either way, remember that a runner runs your functions automatically.

11
00:01:13.157 --> 00:01:19.429
Any tool that changes something in the world needs its own gate, inside the function or in the per-turn hook.

12
00:01:19.709 --> 00:01:21.117
That is lesson 11.
